<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Renewable Energy in Panama</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Why Is Renewable Energy Important for Panama?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Analyze longer narratives. Interactive: Participate in group problem-solving sessions.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> 
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Listening: Can describe key ideas from longer narratives about renewable energy in Panama by summarizing main points and supporting details (e.g., "In the article about Panama's renewable energy projects, they explained how solar panels in rural areas provide electricity to homes, reduce reliance on fossil fuels, and improve daily life for families.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can participate in group problem-solving by sharing ideas and proposing realistic solutions for Panama's energy challenges, including rural areas (e.g., "We can install solar panels in remote villages to provide electricity for schools and reduce reliance on generators.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can actively participate by asking thoughtful questions to explore energy-saving ideas (e.g., "How does wind power help protect the environment?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of new vocabulary and concepts.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing various images of energy sources common in Panama and globally (e.g., a solar panel field, a wind turbine, a hydroelectric dam, an oil rig/fossil fuel power plant). Students are asked to identify them and share what they know about each. Teacher asks, "What kind of energy sources do you see? Which ones are clean? Which ones are not?" Introduces key vocabulary: <i>energy, solar, wind, source, electricity, power, renewable, fossil fuels, environment.</i></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of new vocabulary words, focusing on stress in multisyllabic and compound words (e.g., "renew," "renewable," "energy," "environment," "fossil fuels"). The teacher also models how to form a sentence using a gerund to describe an action, for example: "<i>Saving</i> energy is important for our future." The teacher demonstrates clarifying questions like "What is 'renewable' energy?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary and concepts.
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is coal a <i>renewable energy source</i>? Why or why not?" (Expected answer: "No, because it doesn't renew naturally.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you think is a good way of <i>saving energy</i> in your house?" (Expected answer: "Unplugging devices," "Turning off lights.")</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to "Renewable Energy in Panama". Focus on listening for gist and specific information.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A 2-3 minute audio narrative excerpt (e.g., a short news report or podcast segment) titled "Panama's Green Energy Future" discussing specific renewable energy projects (solar, wind, hydropower) in different regions of Panama, including rural areas. The audio will highlight their importance for sustainability, providing electricity to communities, and reducing reliance on fossil fuels. It will naturally include vocabulary like <i>generate, provide, resource, preservation, sustainability, conservation, panels, turbine, hydropower, efficiency, grid, infrastructure, consumption, reliance</i>.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Receptive: Analyze longer narratives about renewable energy in Panama.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listen):</b> Students listen to the audio once to get the main idea. "What is the main topic of this audio? Is it about new ways Panama is producing electricity, or how much energy Panamanians consume?" (Expected answer: New ways Panama is producing electricity.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listen):</b> Students listen again and decide if the following statements are True or False.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Panama is currently installing more solar panels in rural areas." (True)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The audio mentions that fossil fuels are the best source for future energy." (False)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Wind turbines help to preserve the environment." (True)</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on listening comprehension and targeted language use.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using gerunds to describe environmental actions, present continuous for ongoing actions, adverbial clauses for justification. Reinforcing vocabulary from the audio (e.g., <i>generate, provide, preserve, invest, save, clean, efficient, sustainable, cost-effective</i>).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Listening for Specifics/Gap Fill):</b> Students receive a transcript of the audio with 5-7 key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>generate, provide, preservation, reliance, sustainable</i>) or grammatical structures (e.g., gerunds like <i>saving</i>, present continuous like <i>are installing</i>) blanked out. They listen to the audio a third time and fill in the missing words/phrases. Example: "Panama is actively ______ (installing) more solar panels, ______ (generating) clean electricity."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Completion & Justification):</b> The teacher provides sentence starters related to the audio, focusing on gerunds and adverbial clauses, and students complete them by listening carefully to the context. Examples:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"______ (Investing) in hydropower is good for Panama because ______." (Students listen for the justification in the audio).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We are ______ (working) to reduce fossil fuel ______ (consumption) since ______." (Students listen for the ongoing action and the reason).</li>
                    </ul>
                    Teacher models pronunciation, especially minimal pairs like /e/ in "pen" (e.g., "energy") and /eɪ/ in "pain" (e.g., "maintain," "gain").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students compare their answers from Activities 1 and 2. They take turns reading their completed sentences to each other, listening carefully to identify any discrepancies. They must justify their answers by referencing what they heard in the audio. Example: "I wrote 'installing' because I heard the speaker say 'Panama <i>is installing</i> new panels' at 0:45." This encourages active listening and collaborative problem-solving.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use the language in a realistic scenario: "Renewable Energy in Panama".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group problem-solving discussion.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Task:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students) to discuss a specific energy challenge in Panama, focusing on rural areas, based on what they heard in the audio and their prior knowledge. The teacher presents a prompt: "Imagine your group is advising a local community in rural Panama that currently relies on expensive generators. Based on the audio and what you know, propose two realistic solutions using renewable energy sources. Justify your proposals."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students must actively listen to each other's ideas, ask thoughtful questions (e.g., "How does wind power help protect the environment?"), and use target vocabulary (e.g., <i>solar, wind, power, electricity, provide, efficient, cost-effective</i>) and grammar (e.g., "We can install solar panels in remote villages to provide electricity for schools and reduce reliance on generators." "<i>Investing</i> in small-scale hydropower is a good solution <i>because</i> it uses natural resources.") to explain environmental benefits and justify their opinions. They aim to reach a consensus on the best two solutions.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to gauge learning outcomes.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussion, focusing on active listening, participation, and language use.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their two proposed solutions to the class, outlining their justifications. Students listen to other groups' proposals and can ask one clarifying question.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher checklist focuses on):</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>renewable, sustainable, panels, generate</i>).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining solutions and justifications (e.g., using adverbial clauses).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active listening and collaborative interaction within the group and during presentations.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation by asking thoughtful questions (e.g., "How does wind power help protect the environment?").</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket:
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new fact you learned about renewable energy in Panama from today's audio?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new vocabulary word you used or heard today, and try to use it in a sentence."</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and planning for future lessons.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> Students reflect on their learning:
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did I understand the main ideas and details in the audio today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one challenge I faced while listening or discussing, and how can I improve for next time?"</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., clarity of the audio, engagement with group discussion).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with listening comprehension or using new vocabulary/grammar? (e.g., providing a vocabulary glossary, chunking audio into smaller segments, more guided sentence frames).</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we listened to an audio about renewable energy. In our next lesson, we will read a text that uses some of the same important words and ideas we discussed today, to deepen our understanding of Panama's energy future."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>